    "question": "Can you tell me about a time when you stepped up and demonstrated leadership even when you were not in an official leadership role?",
    "sampleAnswer": "You can use your 3 “short stories” to answer this question, but you should stick to the one\nwhere you did not hold an official title or leadership role.\nMost likely, that means you’ll use your “Success” story or “Failure” story. Here’s an example:\n“In my corporate finance internship last summer, I mostly supported my Manager and Analyst in\nthe Treasury Department.\nWe had to finish end-of-quarter reports, and my Analyst was pulled into a company-wide offsite\nfor several days while my Manager was away because of a family emergency.\nI knew we would miss the reporting deadline for our Cash and liquidity positions, so I contacted\nall the other departments, introduced myself, explained the situation, and got all the required\ntransactions and balances from them.\nThen, I called the Analyst, explained the results so far, and had him and the Manager sign off on\nmy work. They reviewed it when they came back and fixed a few issues, but we were able to\nmeet the reporting deadline.”\nStrengths & Weaknesses\nAll these questions should be easy if you spent time selecting your 3 strengths and 3\nweaknesses, as recommended above.\nThe main challenge is adapting your responses to the many variations they could ask.\nSometimes, you might have to combine your strengths and weaknesses with your three “short\nstories,” your main “story,” or your answers to other questions."
